  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday through next Monday)\nIssued at 1038 PM CDT Sun Sep 27 2026\n\nMid-level flow will shift southwesterly by mid-week, drawing \nmoisture back into the area. This will result in the return of a \nmore humid airmass that we are all too familiar with. Our streak \nof no rain in Nashville will very likely continue under this ridge\nas rain chances remain slim to none. The forecast becomes more \nuncertain starting Friday as the NBM continues to suggest low to \nmedium rain chances returning, but significant model disagreement\nremains. There is a large spread in potential high temperatures \nFriday because of this, too. Ensembles show very different \noutcomes through Saturday, making the forecast during this time a \nlow confidence one. There is much better agreement and signal for \nrain during the Sunday/Monday timeframe with rain chances areawide\nranging from 50-70%. By this point, atmospheric moisture will be \nmuch higher, setting the stage for a good, hopefully soaking rain.\nThe last day Nashville had measurable rainfall was August 26th, \nand even that was very little, less than a tenth of an inch. We \nare likely about to wrap up the hottest and driest September on \nrecord.",
